  "articleReviewNotes": [
    {
      "cards": [
        "theory-15-detail-02",
        "theory-15-detail-03"
      ],
      "review": "The midpoint locus proof includes both inclusions: three parallel planes for containment and two independent half-homotheties/parallelogram for surjectivity. The minimum-distance argument includes the equality case H=Q and distinguishes arbitrary joins from a common perpendicular."
    },
    {
      "cards": [
        "theory-15-detail-04",
        "theory-15-detail-05"
      ],
      "review": "Both printed cube methods are retained. The first correctly uses supporting planes and a diagonal trisected by similarity; it explicitly says the feet need not lie on the original lines. The second proves the projection method for arbitrary skew lines, invariance under shifting the perpendicular plane, then calculates the cube distance using a right-triangle altitude."
    },
    {
      "cards": [
        "theory-19-detail-02",
        "theory-19-detail-03",
        "theory-19-detail-04",
        "theory-19-detail-05"
      ],
      "review": "All parts of the regular-polyhedron definition are included: convexity, congruent regular faces and uniform vertex degree. Five actual models exist, with tetrahedron linked to the previously reviewed theory18-detail04. Independent calculations establish all face angles, edge lengths, convex supporting planes, manifold incidence, vertex links and Euler counts. Natural objects are explicitly approximations rather than assertions of exact regularity."
    },
    {
      "cards": [
        "theory-19-detail-06"
      ],
      "review": "The strict convex vertex-angle bound is proved by cutting off the vertex and applying the trihedral angle inequality from project3, whose supplied proof was separately reread. The five integer pairs follow correctly. Necessary incidence conditions are explicitly distinguished from constructing existing solids and from a separate rigidity/uniqueness theorem."
    },
    {
      "cards": [
        "theory-19-detail-07",
        "theory-19-detail-08"
      ],
      "review": "All open/bounded/adherent definitions use three-dimensional positive-radius balls. Interior and exterior witness radii are explicit; endpoints of an open interval are correctly adherent but excluded. Wire circles are captioned as approximate boundary sections, not solid balls. Nonempty planar figures are correctly nonopen in ambient space."
    },
    {
      "cards": [
        "theory-19-detail-09",
        "theory-19-detail-10"
      ],
      "review": "Connectedness uses nonempty disjoint partitions and adherence; the optional interval proof identifies its completeness argument. Boundary requires adherence to both figure and complement. The body definition retains boundedness and a nonempty connected open generating set plus its whole boundary. F is disconnected; G is connected but has disconnected interior, so both fail the body definition for distinct reasons. All source examples and historical context are addressed."
    }
  ],
